WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.640
If you're living your life&nbsp;
right, you've already finished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.560
every episode of Jordan Blum and Patton&nbsp;
Oswalt's Hulu series "Marvel's MODOK."

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.200
Across 10 episodes, the show&nbsp;
brings viewers into the life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.520
of the man described as comics' most iconic&nbsp;
Mechanized Organism Designed Only for Killing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.600
and draws the curtain back on his problematic&nbsp;
approach to finding a work-life balance.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.720
This take on MODOK is a reimagining far removed&nbsp;
from his usual appearances in Marvel Comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.160
Sure, there's cackling and a healthy&nbsp;
dose of super-science machinations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.240
but his villainous behavior is presented&nbsp;
less as an alien philosophy and more like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.920
the pursuits of a guy in his 40s who's still&nbsp;
trying to make it as a stand up comedian.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.040
MODOK might have noble intentions but&nbsp;
they're rooted in the same delusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.520
that turns otherwise capable adults into&nbsp;
starry-eyed monuments to arrested development.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.800
"For the millionth time, it won't&nbsp;
work. You can't have it all."

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.360
"Yes I can — I'm very special."

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.080
His moral gradient is black and white:&nbsp;
There are good people, like MODOK's mom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.200
who told him that he was special.&nbsp;
Everyone else falls into the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.960
category as the bullies who beat him up.&nbsp;
Their behavior will need to be corrected.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.000
"Hey, we're people!"

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:04.640
"Not to me!"

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.960
It's a flailing, simplistic worldview,&nbsp;
illustrated by MODOK's dramatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.080
all-or-nothing way of dealing&nbsp;
with, well, literally everything.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.600
Throughout the show's 10 episodes, MODOK&nbsp;
takes the baby steps appropriate for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.200
man of his head-to-leg ratio, thanks&nbsp;
in large part to his forced reckoning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.040
with family life. Here's how "Marvel's&nbsp;
MODOK" wraps up, and what it all means.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.840
As the series progresses, little by little,&nbsp;
MODOK makes a gradual transition towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.080
being a better father. He's still not great&nbsp;
in the end, but you know what? He's okay.&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.560
He dances with his son Lou at his bar mitzvah,&nbsp;
acknowledges his shortcomings to his wife Jodie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.320
and even elicits a positive emotion from&nbsp;
his daughter Melissa, which is difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.640
when you're parenting any teenage girl, let&nbsp;
alone one with a dominant supervillain gene.&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.280
But MODOK is still haunted by the man he was&nbsp;
20 years ago, and in this case, it's literal:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.440
the time-displaced MODOK from the past finds&nbsp;
his present self and reminds him of what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.520
wanted to be. Past-MODOK shows his older&nbsp;
self all of the futures that could occur:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.200
deaths at the hands of every Avenger,&nbsp;
toilet heart attacks, and, presciently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.760
that one potential outcome that sees MODOK&nbsp;
become the ruler of a utopian, unified Earth.&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.800
"You're a supervillain. You hate the world!"

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.280
"No I don't. I just want to change it."

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.240
It's a strange setup that calls back to Doctor&nbsp;
Strange's Infinity War scenario of "one chance in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.360
14 million for victory," with one dark difference.&nbsp;
In the Infinity War, Tony Stark has to come to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.920
terms with how much of himself he'll sacrifice,&nbsp;
while MODOK can succeed only if he sacrifices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.800
others -- specifically, the family that he worked&nbsp;
so hard to deserve. The key word there -- the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.080
that cemented the family's doom -- is "worked."

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.440
It's unavoidable that "Marvel's MODOK"&nbsp;
is going to be compared to Adult Swim's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.120
"Rick and Morty," as they both have science&nbsp;
fiction elements, pop culture references,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.880
and self-serving cartoon geniuses. So it's&nbsp;
fitting that a line from Rick and Morty can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.760
sum up the general theme of MODOK season 1.
"The thing about repairing, maintaining, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:56.480
cleaning is, it's not an adventure. There's no way&nbsp;
to do it so wrong you might die. It's just work."&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.520
That's the conundrum at the center of "MODOK":&nbsp;
take the exciting, destructive, dramatic path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.600
or keep working at the day-to-day aspects&nbsp;
of life. It's the ol' "Kill Iron Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.040
or clean your apartment" conundrum.
One option means that MODOK's mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.480
was right, and he's a very special&nbsp;
boy. The other means doing the work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.480
and, if his younger self is to be believed,&nbsp;
never becoming what he dreamed he would be.&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.560
Melissa's earlier attempts to teach her old man&nbsp;
how to take the subtle, effective route echo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.040
through his decision, and MODOK stands at the same&nbsp;
proverbial fork in the road as every dad that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.720
ever thought about just getting in the car and&nbsp;
driving away from it all, with the slight addendum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.520
of a dozen rockets being fired at his kids.
Then comes the flash forward: MODOK chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.920
the first option. Like Doctor Horrible before&nbsp;
him, MODOK has everything he's ever wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.320
at the cost of the lives of the people he loved.
Yes, he's keeping his younger self tethered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.880
to a torture device, using his chronocrystal&nbsp;
facial bedazzling to look back on what he lost.&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.720
But wanting his family back doesn't mean that he&nbsp;
fixed the part of himself that threw them away to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.680
begin with. Untold years and a few dozen Avenger&nbsp;
deaths later, and grand, dramatic gestures are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.080
still MODOK's only approach to problem-solving.&nbsp;
Like the darkest possible iteration of Murphy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.600
Brown, MODOK declares that he will have it all.
How that'll pan out remains to be seen. Series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.360
co-creator Jordan Blum has hinted that&nbsp;
a second season is already planned out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.080
so fans should get excited for more "MODOK"&nbsp;
misadventures in the (hopefully near) future.&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.000
"Yeah, let's just get out of here."
